{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"reasoning","text":"Python 3.14.5 is available, with pandas 3.0.5, numpy 2.5.2, scipy 1.18.1.\n\nOK, let me lay out the analysis plan:\n\n**Task:** Analyze enhancer-promoter 3D distances and transcription dynamics across cells and over time.\n\n**Outputs:**\n1. `output/cell_metrics.csv` — columns: `cell_id,n_timepoints,mean_distance_nm,contact_fraction,transcription_fraction`\n   - Euclidean distance from x/y/z coordinates (in nm units)\n   - Contact = distance ≤ 260 nm\n   - Fractions use all 250 rows per cell\n\n2. `output/lag_analysis.csv` — one row for every integer lag from -20 to +20, columns: `lag,association,n_observations`\n   - association = pooled Pearson correlation across all cells between contact at time t and transcription at time t+lag\n   - Positive lag means contact leads the later transcription value\n   - Do not join observations across cell boundaries\n\n3. `output/analysis.py` — the analysis script\n\n4. `output/report.md` — the report\n\n**Interpretation requirements:**\n- Use 260 nm as the contact threshold\n- Distinguish temporal association from causation, and state explicitly what the observational data cannot establish\n\nLet me think about \"pooled Pearson correlation across all cells.\" There are two natural interpretations:\n\n1. Pool all (contact_t, transcription_{t+lag}) pairs from all cells into one big array and compute a single Pearson correlation. \"Pooled\" most naturally means this: concatenate observations across cells and compute one correlation. The rule says \"do not join observations across cell boundaries\" — this means when forming pairs (t, t+lag), both must come from the same cell; but the correlation itself is computed on the pooled data.\n\n2. Compute per-cell correlations and average them.\n\nThe word \"pooled\" strongly suggests interpretation 1: pool the observations and compute Pearson correlation on the pooled data. The constraint \"do not join observations across cell boundaries\" clarifies that pairing must be within cells, but pooling for the correlation is fine.\n\nLet me go with interpretation 1: for each lag, build pairs (contact at t, transcription at t+lag) within each cell, pool across cells, compute Pearson r. n_observations = number of such pooled pairs.\n\nLet me think about the contact variable. Contact is binary (distance ≤ 260 → 1, else 0). Transcription is also binary (0/1 in the data). Pearson correlation between two binary variables is the phi coefficient. That's fine.\n\nLet me think about \"contact at time t\" — the contact indicator derived from distance at time t.\n\nFor each lag L in -20..20:\n- For each cell, for each time t such that both t and t+L are in [0, 249], get the pair (contact[t], transcription[t+L]).\n- Pool across cells.\n- Compute Pearson correlation.\n- n_observations = total number of pairs.\n\nFor positive lag L, contact at earlier time t is paired with transcription at later time t+L — \"contact leads the later transcription value.\" Good.\n\nFor negative lag L, contact at time t is paired with transcription at earlier time t+L (i.e., transcription precedes contact).\n\nLet me write the analysis script. First, do some exploratory data analysis to understand transcription dynamics and write a good report.\n\nActually, let me write a comprehensive analysis.py that does everything and produces the outputs. But first, let me do some quick exploration to inform the report. I'll run some quick computations.\n\nFirst let me quickly check the data for sanity — e.g., transcription values, distance ranges.\n"}}
