transcription {best_lag} step(s) later\"\nelse:\n    causal_read = f\"transcription causes contact {-best_lag} step(s) later\"\n```\n\n(h) Fix for set95 formatting: display as \"lag -1\" / \"lags -2..0\" — format as a comma-separated string with signed lags: `\", \".join(f\"{l:+d}\" for l in set95_lags)`.\n\nAlso (i): the sentence in Section 3.2 Key findings: \"transcription at t−1 is followed by contact at time t: transcription tends to *precede* contact.\" Good.\n\nAnd the conditional probability explanation paragraph is generic, using cond[best_lag] — no problem, but \"followed by contact\" only holds when best_lag=-1; the generic sentence \"transcription at t{best_lag:+d} occurs at time points followed by contact in 0.272 of cases\" — for best_lag=-1 it reads as \"transcription at t-1 occurs in 0.272 of time points followed by contact\". OK, but let's generalize: phrase it as \"P(tx=1 at t{best_lag:+d} | contact at t) = ... vs baseline ...\". To be safe, rewrite this sentence generically using the numbers from the conditional table.\n\nApply these patches to analysis.py with a Python patch script (string replacements), rerun, and verify.\n\nAlso, in the \"Key findings\" section, fix the first bullet too: no problem.\n\nOne more check — `ascii_profile` uses `float(v) / rmax * half`, and rmax = max|r| = 0.0778; each '#' ≈ 0.0026. No problem.\n\nAnd Section 3.1: \"Contact is transient/rare relative to the full trajectory: on average, cells are in contact at only a few percent of timepoints.\" — mean contact fraction 5.3%, good.\n\nAlso, the report lists `n_observations` as 600 x (250 − |L|) — correct.\n\nSo, write the patch. Given the number of edits, I'll do a Python script with several precise replacements. Need to be careful that the old strings match exactly. To avoid escaping issues, let me write the patch script to a temp file… a PowerShell here-string containing Python's replacements — Python contains both quote characters. I'll write the patcher in a single